# Dagric OS — User applications
# The rule: ship what a normal person needs on day one, nothing else.
# Everything beyond this comes from Discover (Flatpak), chosen BY the user.

# Web
firefox-esr

# Office — the three components people actually use, not the full suite
libreoffice-writer
libreoffice-calc
libreoffice-impress
libreoffice-kf6

# THE USER INTERFACE OF THESE TWO, IN THE LANGUAGES THIS OS ALREADY SPEAKS.
#
# The dictionaries below were the second half of this problem, not the whole of
# it, and fixing them made the remaining half look finished. Read the Aug-4 Pro
# ISO and the truth was: 544 German .mo catalogues on the image, plasmashell,
# dolphin, konsole and systemsettings among them, so the DESKTOP is German —
# and /usr/lib/libreoffice/share/registry/ held exactly one langpack file,
# Langpack-en-US.xcd, with no locale packs under firefox-esr at all.
#
# So a German owner got a German desktop, German Dagric tools, and German red
# underlines inside an English Writer. That is the worst of the three possible
# states: fully English would at least be consistent, and fully German would be
# right. Half-translated reads as broken, and it reads that way in Writer and
# Firefox specifically — the two applications this audience spends its day in.
#
# These are Suggests, not Recommends, so 01dagric-norecommends is not the cause
# here and removing it would not have helped; apt never installs Suggests. They
# have to be named.
#
# NAMES CHECKED WITH `apt-get install -s`, NOT `apt-cache show`. Spanish has no
# firefox-esr-l10n-es or thunderbird-l10n-es — only es-es, es-ar, es-cl, es-mx —
# while LibreOffice does use a plain -es. Guessing a symmetric set would have
# put two nonexistent names in this file and rolled the build back an hour in,
# which is exactly how signon-ui went. apt-cache show is NOT the test: it exits
# 0 with empty output for a name that only appears as a dependency reference,
# so it calls signon-ui present. install -s calls it absent, correctly.
#
# 12.6 MB for both editions. The l10n packages are UI strings only; offline
# help is a separate libreoffice-help-* set and is deliberately not taken.
firefox-esr-l10n-de
firefox-esr-l10n-es-es
firefox-esr-l10n-fr
firefox-esr-l10n-it
firefox-esr-l10n-pt-br
libreoffice-l10n-de
libreoffice-l10n-es
libreoffice-l10n-fr
libreoffice-l10n-it
libreoffice-l10n-pt-br

# SPELL CHECK IN THE LANGUAGES THIS OS ACTUALLY SPEAKS.
#
# hunspell-en-us arrives already, as a dependency of LibreOffice — confirmed by
# "Setting up hunspell-en-us" in the build logs. Nothing else does, and the
# dictionaries for other languages are Recommends, which
# /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/01dagric-norecommends drops on the image and on every
# installed machine afterwards.
#
# Dagric deliberately ships five languages: the wizard, the Hub, the helpers and
# the manual are translated into de, fr, es, pt_BR and it, and the user guide has
# a full German edition. So a German owner gets a German desktop, a German guide
# — and no red underline anywhere in Writer, in the one application people
# switch to Linux to write letters in. That reads as "the spell check is broken",
# not as "leaner".
#
# Names checked against the trixie archive rather than guessed: German and French
# both have several variants (hunspell-de-de vs -at/-ch, hunspell-fr-classical vs
# -revised), and hunspell-fr is the dependency package that pulls a real one.
# About 1-2 MB each.
hunspell-de-de
hunspell-fr
hunspell-es
hunspell-it
hunspell-pt-br

# Media — one player that plays everything
mpv

# Music library — mpv plays files, but a switcher expects a real music app
# with a library, playlists, and album art. Elisa is KDE's own: small, no
# accounts, no cloud. (Audit: free had no music library at all.)
elisa
